<%# ONE SUGGESTION: its sentence, and the control that acts on it. Locals: `suggestion`
    (SuggestionEngine::Suggestion), `presenter`.

    The sentence is per kind (§8's table is the copy target) and lives in its own partial; what is
    shared is the frame, the accept control and the two clauses every PROPOSING kind carries — what
    accepting does to the category, and which cap it would replace. Those two are here rather than
    in each kind's partial precisely so a bill and a rate cannot come to describe the same act in
    two different ways.

    `data-suggestion` is kind AND subject id: a category and a pool may share a name, and
    `Capybara.exact` is unset, so a name-keyed row is one rename away from matching two. %>
<article class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0" data-suggestion="<%= suggestion_key(suggestion) %>">
  <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between gap-3">
    <div class="text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-1">
      <%= render "budget_page/suggestion_#{suggestion.kind}", suggestion: suggestion %>

      <% joined = presenter.joined_envelope_name(suggestion) %>
      <% if suggestion_re_points_category?(suggestion) %>
        <%# THE RE-POINT, SAID BEFORE THE CLICK. Accepting a $45 Parking proposal moves EVERY
            Transportation entry into the envelope — the gas, the rideshares, all of it — and a
            user who was not told that has had several hundred dollars of unrelated flow moved for
            them. `Budget#item_must_belong_to_pool` is why it has to happen at all: a rule's item
            must sit in a category pointing at the rule's pool.

            WHETHER THE ENVELOPE IS NEW IS A SEPARATE QUESTION from whether the category moves, and
            the two are said separately because they are two acts. A user who already has an
            envelope by that name is JOINING it, not creating a second — the demo's "Utilities" is
            exactly that shape — and a sentence promising a new one would be describing something
            the save is not going to do.

            "PAST AND FUTURE", AND IT IS A CLAUSE RATHER THAN A PARAGRAPH. `ENTRY_POOL_ID` has no
            date bound and `PoolCalculator#balance` is start-date-agnostic, so the re-point moves
            the category's WHOLE history: a new envelope opens at minus its lifetime spend — the
            demo's read `overdrawn $496.00` — and the user who was not told reads a red row they
            did nothing to earn. Three warnings on one row is one too many, though, and this is the
            least of them: burying the re-point or the cap it deletes would be worse than omitting
            this. So it rides on the sentence already saying which spending moves.

            THE TWO BRANCHES SAY IT DIFFERENTLY BECAUSE THEY DO DIFFERENT THINGS. A NEW envelope
            OPENS at minus the lifetime spend, which is a starting balance. A JOINED one already
            exists and already holds money; the re-point DROPS that balance by the same figure, so
            it "takes on" the history rather than starting out with it — and it is the join whose
            resulting number is least predictable from anything else on the row. %>
        <p class="text-gray-500" data-suggestion-effect>
          <% if joined %>
            This rule joins your existing <%= joined %> envelope, and points all
            <%= suggestion.detail[:category_name] || suggestion.subject.name %> spending at it,
            past and future — so the envelope takes on what has already been spent.
          <% else %>
            Accepting this puts all <%= suggestion.detail[:category_name] || suggestion.subject.name %>
            spending in a new <%= suggestion_proposed_envelope_name(suggestion) %> envelope,
            past and future — so it opens carrying what has already been spent.
          <% end %>
        </p>

        <% cap = presenter.cap_for(suggestion) %>
        <% if cap %>
          <%# THE CAP THIS WOULD DESTROY, named with its own figure. A category cannot hold both a
              cap and a pool, so `Category#destroy_budget_if_pool_linked` deletes the cap the
              moment the re-point lands. The trade is honest — a cap is a spending limit that
              reserves nothing (`Budget.steady_need` counts pool-mode rules only) and the rule
              replacing it is a real claim on the period's income — but it is a DELETION of something the
              user wrote, so it is stated rather than discovered. %>
          <p class="text-gray-500" data-suggestion-cap>
            Your <%= number_to_currency(cap.amount) %> <%= budget_rule_basis(cap) %> cap here is a spending limit,
            not a claim on your income — nothing fills one — and accepting this replaces it with a rule that is.
          </p>
        <% end %>
      <% elsif joined %>
        <%# THE SHARING CASE, and it is the whole reason the envelope is the CATEGORY's rather than
            the item's. Phone, Internet and Streaming Services are three bills in one Utilities
            category: the first acceptance settles the envelope, and from then on the engine's own
            payload reuses it (the category is pool-covered), so this rule ADDS to the envelope
            rather than making a second one — and the category does not move again. %>
        <p class="text-gray-500" data-suggestion-effect>
          This rule joins your existing <%= joined %> envelope, alongside what is already in it.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="shrink-0 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
      <%= link_to suggestion_accept_label(suggestion),
                  suggestion_accept_path(suggestion),
                  class: "btn btn-primary whitespace-nowrap",
                  data: { suggestion_accept: true } %>

      <% if suggestion.kind == :dead_rule %>
        <%# THE PAGE DOES NOT DELETE — the user does. Both doors are open on the row because the
            decision is genuinely theirs: a bill that stopped because the subscription was
            cancelled wants the rule gone, and one that stopped because the card was replaced wants
            it kept. Offering only "review" would hide the answer most dead rules deserve; deleting
            on "accept" would take a rule off a money screen on one click. %>
        <%= button_to "Delete the rule",
                      budget_path(suggestion.subject),
                      method: :delete,
                      class: "btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap",
                      form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Delete this rule? Nothing else about #{suggestion.detail[:item_name]} changes." } } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
